n ICO you  need someone who knows how to run  investment projects right and that  that's something that is not always  there for some of these projects Peter  David is the non-executive director he  has an experienced lifetime of  experience in the tech business world  and then Jayne Sadler kid  another non-executive director pretty  diverse group of people from a number of  different areas of business token sale  allocation 60% of the tokens are going  to the contributors there's going to be  a 1% airdrop and a 5% bounty program and  then 10% of the tokens will be  distributed to advisers 24 percent will  be kept by sharing so not an altogether  unusual allocation and the as sharing  has made clear the sharing Treasury  itself will be publicly auditable and  the public address of the cryptocurrency  wallets is going to be published on the  share ring website allocation of tokens  after the ICO is going to be roughly as  follows although they are going to  present the full budget during the  mainsail 30% for the main net  development 10% for external reviews and  tech validation by third parties 30% for  incubators and bonuses to get working on  third-party product integrations and  that I mean if you think about make or  break here right it's buying it's our  people seeing the value and are they  going to use this and incubating the  third product integrations it's not like  a nice-to-have it's an absolute and  total necessity 20% for global marketing  and promotions and then 10% for  contingency funds they will lock up  unsold tokens pre token sharing event  February 2018 the pre TSE ends May 2018  and then the TSE period until June 4th  2018 which is where we are now  a number of different contribution tiers  which you can read about don't have to  go through that in detail now so big  picture we've talked a lot about the  fragmentation that exists in the crypto  world number one this specifically is an  ICO that's looking at fragmentation  within the sharing economy so the idea  is fantastic the challenge is you need  the buy-in right you need to have enough  industry participants so that someone  would say okay I can get housing rent a  car as well as other services for my  trip all through the sharing system such  that it's obviously more convenient to  do that than to use Airbnb for one thing  and then uber for something else  etc etc the number one factor assuming  of course that the technology is in  place and I think the technology is  actually it's ambitious but they have  the team to do the technology part it's  going to be all about buy-in and that's  why I want to see as much focus as  possible being put on bringing in those  third party partners because that's  ultimately what it's going to come down  to nothing in this review is meant to be  considered investment advice everybody  should do their own research if they're  going to make any financial decision  it's a review of the project and my  thoughts on the possibility of the  project success and you can read more  about the sharing ICO and project by  looking at the links in the video  description below
 

